WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.950
Before you start: preparing the academy. D2.4 Implementation Guide · Video 1 of 4 ·
Chapter 2.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:16.252
Why start here. Academies that started with a clear problem, a named data owner and
a realistic budget kept using their tools; others did not. Technology projects often

3
00:00:16.302 --> 00:00:18.950
fail before the first device is switched on.

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:29.144
Five decisions to take first. 1  Start with a specific problem. 2  Check your
academy's digital readiness. 3  Plan budget and staff time. 4  Name the roles. 5

5
00:00:29.194 --> 00:00:30.950
Protect young players' data.

6
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.717
1  Start with a specific problem. Write down the one problem you want to solve this
season. For example: "Our U15 scouts rate the same player very differently." "We do

7
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:46.299
not know how hard last week was for each player." "Players do not understand our
feedback on positioning." Then write how you will know it is solved. Use something

8
00:00:46.349 --> 00:00:46.950
you can count.

9
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.950
Check. Your problem statement fits in two sentences, and a colleague can repeat it
back to you.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.578
2  Digital-readiness self-assessment. Seven questions (Annex B): people, time,
equipment, internet, data handling, budget, player and parent trust. Starting:

11
00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:07.417
Session-RPE, a phone on a tripod for video, consent routine first. Developing: One
technology, one age group, one season. Established: Add a second technology and

12
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.950
combine the data. The bands are a planning aid, not a score.

13
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:19.482
3  Budget, staff time and sustainable tools. Staff time is the cost academies forget
to budget; licence fees are only the visible part. Choose tools you can sustain for

14
00:01:19.532 --> 00:01:25.950
three seasons. Remove repetitive paperwork first. Pilot before you commit: one age
group, one season, then decide.

15
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:34.950
Small-academy option. Session-RPE instead of sensors. A phone camera instead of an
automated camera. A structured scouting template instead of an AI tool.

16
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.154
4  Roles and responsibilities. One name and a backup for each role. Data owner (per
tool): Setup, download, quality check, storage and access. Interpreter: A short

17
00:01:42.204 --> 00:01:49.536
report with one clear message. Feedback lead: What players and parents are told, and
how. Decision maker: Always a person, never a tool. Data protection contact: Legal

18
00:01:49.586 --> 00:01:50.950
basis, consent, retention, DPIA.

19
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.422
5  Data protection for youth players. Decide the legal basis first, before you
collect any data. Involve parents for minors. Treat sensor data as possible health

20
00:01:57.472 --> 00:02:04.096
data. A person takes every decision about a young player. Before posting any clip
online: a clear legal basis, where and for how long, and the right to withdraw. This

21
00:02:04.146 --> 00:02:06.950
is not legal advice. Confirm each point with your DPO or legal adviser.

22
